  "hypothesis": {
    "id": "hazard-rate-vs-primary-decorrelation-audit",
    "parent": "recovery-anchored-to-own-inflight-sends",
    "kind": "meta",
    "title": "Audit whether h-rate/acted-fraction deltas have ever predicted primary-objective deltas",
    "description": "Offline analysis over the stored evaluation records (no simulator runs, no spur build). For every hypothesis with a recorded objectiveDeltas block, extract (h1, h2, h3, throughput) deltas and the primary/depth>=N deltas, and compute rank correlation plus a sign-agreement rate. Emit the result as a short note the pool's fire-criterion guidance can cite. Concretely: if sign agreement between 'h-rate up' and 'primary up' is at or below chance, then any future add whose ONLY pre-registered fire criterion is a hazard rate or an acted fraction must additionally pre-register a depth-band criterion, or be downgraded before it is queued. Also report the reverse cell (primary up with h-rates flat/down), which identifies which past winners moved the ladder WITHOUT moving hazards — that set is the real generator for the next family.",
    "category": "feedback",
    "buildsOn": [
      "deliveryEffects acted counters",
      "sequential evaluation records",
      "objectiveDeltas"
    ],
    "expectedGain": 3,
    "expectedCost": 0.4,
    "rationale": "This run is the cleanest counterexample yet to the pool's implicit theory of change: h1/h2/h3 all rose while every depth band fell. Six or seven hypotheses in this family were justified by an acted-fraction asymmetry that has now been shown to be real AND non-predictive. Auditing the whole record turns one anecdote into a queueing rule and stops the family from regenerating under a new name.",
    "generalityArgument": "Pure retrospective statistics over already-recorded objective deltas; it references no protocol, no message content, and no VR-specific mechanism. The output is a rule about how hypotheses are gated, applicable to any spec under evaluation.",
